# Local development. Copy to .env and fill in.
# Production values are set by scripts/deploy.sh; see DEPLOYMENT.md.

# --- Local server --------------------------------------------------------
# The port `npm run dev` listens on. Cloud Run sets this itself, so it is a local convenience
# rather than something deploy.sh passes.
PORT="3000"

# --- Identity -------------------------------------------------------------
# iap  = verify the X-Goog-IAP-JWT-Assertion header (production; the default)
# dev  = trust DEV_USER_* below. Refused when NODE_ENV=production.
AUTH_MODE="dev"
DEV_USER_EMAIL="you@example.com"
DEV_USER_NAME="Your Name"

# Set by deploy.sh in production; format is
#   /projects/PROJECT_NUMBER/locations/REGION/services/SERVICE_NAME
# Required whenever AUTH_MODE=iap. Leave empty locally.
IAP_AUDIENCE=""

# --- Storage --------------------------------------------------------------
# Empty locally: engagements fall back to a JSON file under .data/.
# Set to a project id in production to use Firestore.
FIRESTORE_PROJECT_ID=""

# --- Model ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Two separate questions, and keeping them apart is the point.
#
# MODEL_BACKEND is *how the client authenticates*:
#   apikey = each provider the chain names uses its own key below (easiest for local dev)
#   vertex = ADC as the runtime service account (production; no key material at all)
#
# Vertex serves BOTH providers under that same ADC, which is why running Claude is a model
# decision and not a security-posture decision. See docs/intents/004.
MODEL_BACKEND="apikey"

# One key per provider, and deliberately NOT one neutral MODEL_API_KEY: with two providers
# there are two keys, and a single variable could not say which one it held. A key is the one
# setting here whose provider name was never a lie. Only the ones your chain names are needed.
#
# Left as its placeholder, a key counts as unset and that provider is simply unreachable. With
# none reachable the app runs with no model at all — every route answers with a labelled
# fallback, which is why a fresh clone works with nothing configured.
GEMINI_API_KEY="MY_GEMINI_API_KEY"
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=""

# --- Deprecated model names -----------------------------------------------
# These named a provider in a place that is no longer provider-specific. They still work and
# are read when the current name is unset, with one warning at boot — which is what lets the
# revision deployed *before* the rename keep running. scripts/deploy.sh now pushes only the
# current names, so a redeployed service stops carrying these at all.
#
#   GENAI_BACKEND -> MODEL_BACKEND
#   GEMINI_MODELS -> MODEL_CHAIN
#
# The API keys are not on this list, and that is the point of the note above.
GENAI_BACKEND=""

# vertex only. Where the model is served from — a region id, or "global".
#
# `global` routes to whatever region has capacity, so it takes fewer 429s and gets new model
# ids first; for stable versions the per-token price is the same either way. A region id
# pins where prompts are served, which is what you want if a client requires residency.
# scripts/deploy.sh currently passes $REGION here, so a deployment inherits the Cloud Run
# region unless you change it.
#
# Not to be confused with Vertex Flex PayGo, which is genuinely half price, global-only, and
# targets 1-15 MINUTE responses. That is unusable for an interactive prompt.
VERTEX_LOCATION=""

# Who answers, in order, as comma-separated `provider:model` entries. Empty uses the built-in
# default, which is Gemini-only on purpose: a default that reached for Claude would change
# what an existing deployment does on its next restart.
#
#   default            gemini:gemini-3.5-flash,gemini:gemini-3.5-flash-lite
#   Claude, falling back to Gemini
#                      claude:claude-opus-5,gemini:gemini-3.5-flash
#
# A bare id means Gemini, so every old GEMINI_MODELS value keeps working unchanged. An entry
# naming an unknown provider is dropped with a warning rather than throwing — the point of a
# chain is that it survives one member being wrong.
#
# The chain can cross providers, and entries whose provider has no credentials are skipped
# without a round trip. So naming both on a deployment that only has one set of keys is a
# normal state, not an error.
#
# **Verify these before trusting them.** Valid ids differ between backends and are retired on
# published dates. The Gemini default was re-checked against the Vertex release notes on
# 2026-09-10 (3.5-flash retires no earlier than 2027-05-19, 3.5-flash-lite 2027-07-21). It
# had once been a chain whose fallback tier was shut down three months earlier with nothing
# here noticing.
#
# The failure mode is quiet and costs latency rather than erroring cleanly: a retired id
# answers 404, which is not fatal to the chain, so the request tries the next entry and only
# then fails. Two dead ids means two round trips per request to reach "No model answered".
MODEL_CHAIN=""
GEMINI_MODELS=""
